{{Quote|I can take out the guards at the perimeter. Signalman, pilot, hell I'll just take them all out.|Val, to Tobias Beckett|<ref name="Solo" />}}
'''Val''' was a [[Gender|female]] [[human]] criminal who [[Beckett's gang|worked]] with fellow outlaw [[Tobias Beckett]] during the [[Imperial Era|reign]] of the [[Galactic Empire]]. Val was a no-nonsense and occasionally prickly woman who was a crack shot with a [[blaster rifle]].

==Biography==
Val was highly secretive about her past, including her full name.<ref name="Official Guide" /> All that was known about her past was that she grew up on [[Solarine]]<ref name="Expanded Edition" /> and her [[Val's father|father]] was a [[musician]], and she was named after the [[valachord]] musical instrument.<ref name="Official Guide" />

By the [[Imperial Era|reign]] of the [[Galactic Empire]], Val was already working with the scoundrel [[Tobias Beckett]] and the [[Ardennian]] [[pilot]] [[Rio Durant]].<ref name="Solo" /> They formed a [[Beckett's gang|crew]] which carried out risky though profitable heists.<ref name="Beckett Databank">{{Databank|tobias-beckett|Tobias Beckett}}</ref> [[Gender|She]] and Beckett, in addition, shared a romantic relationship.<ref name="Solo" />

===Rescue of Beckett===
[[File:Val_and_Beckett_sunset.png|thumb|left|175px|Val and Beckett enjoy the sunset together]]
Val infiltrated the [[Winds of Ruin base|base]] of the [[Winds of Ruin]] to [[Rescue of Tobias Beckett|rescue]] Beckett who was imprisoned by the criminals. During the escape, Beckett freed the [[musician]] [[Tu-Wane Jipps]] who owed a debt to Beckett. Beckett threatened to turn Jipps over to the [[Hutt]]s is he did not help him. Val provided cover fire while Beckett helped the musician to the [[hangar]]s. In the hangar the trio was cornered by the Ruin until [[Rio Durant]] arrived to provide the group with a means of escape.<ref name="Credits">{{IDWAdventuresCite-2020|issue=4|story=Tales of Villainy: Credits}}</ref>

On the [[starship]], Val was angry at Beckett for putting her in danger just to save one of his [[gambling]] buddies. But Beckett promised to Val that he had a surprise for her. When the ship landed Beckett brought out Val and surprised her with a relaxing sunset picnic and [[music]] provided by Jipps. Val called Beckett "fantastic" as the couple enjoyed the view.<ref name="Credits" />

===Meeting with Han Solo===
{{Quote|None of your business company. And we're full up.|Val, to Han Solo, when asked what company she was with|<ref name="Solo" />}}
[[File:Val_AtG.png|thumb|right|250px|Val disguised as a mudtrooper on Mimban]]
[[10 BBY|Ten]] [[Standard year|years]] before the [[Battle of Yavin]], during a [[Mimban Campaign|war]] on [[Mimban]], Val infiltrated the [[Wet-weather gear stormtrooper|Mimban Stormtrooper]] [[troop]]s along with Beckett and Durant. There, they met the young [[Han Solo]], who wanted to join them as part of their crew. Val didn't agree in letting Solo join them, but Beckett decided to give the boy an opportunity by leading him to be locked for insubordination. Solo was ultimately able to escape with the [[Wookiee]] [[Chewbacca]], who was initially intended to devour them, so Beckett decided to let them join his crew.<ref name="Solo" />

Once they arrived at [[Vandor]], Beckett's crew spied the [[20-T Railcrawler conveyex transport]] that they had been planning to steal in order to acquire its [[coaxium]] cargo to pay Beckett's debt with the [[crime lord]] [[Dryden Vos]], leader of [[Crimson Dawn]]. As the crew spied, they wished for the absence of [[Enfys Nest]] and the [[Cloud-Riders]] the next [[Standard day|day]]. That night, before sleeping with the campfire they had made, Val kissed Beckett while they told about what they planned to do with their share from the heist.<ref name="Solo" />

===The Conveyex heist and death===
[[File:Val_HiRes.jpg|thumb|left|200px|Val on Vandor]]
The next morning, while Beckett, Solo, and Chewbacca managed to take possession of the cargo wagon by getting rid of [[range trooper]]s and separating some of the cars, Val prepared to detonate a bridge to allow the crew's stolen [[Y-45 armored transport hauler|AT hauler]] to lift one of the conveyex cars away as the rest of the train fell into a ravine.<ref name="Solo" />

Unfortunately, Nest and the Cloud-Riders appeared on scene to steal the coaxium from Beckett's hands, managing to fatally wound Rio as he piloted the hauler. As Solo fought to gain control of the ship, a security sensor was tripped, alerting several [[11-3K viper probe droid|viper probe droids]]. Though Val managed to use her [[EC-17 hold-out blaster]] to take out some of the droids, she found herself pinned, realizing that the conveyex was getting too close to allow time to abandon her position to join the others. Communicating with Beckett about the change of plans, she told Beckett that she loved him and subsequently detonated the bomb, [[Death|blowing herself up]] and destroying the bridge.<ref name="Solo" />

===Legacy===
[[File:Val-SASWSOCE.png|thumb|right|180px|Val was well equipped for any mission.]]
Ultimately, Val's sacrifice was in vain as Solo was forced to let the cargo go in order to save Beckett, Chewie, and himself from certain death. Once all had finished, Beckett punched Solo in the face because of Val's death and their failure in acquiring the coaxium. Later on, while visiting Vos with the empty hands, Vos expressed his condolences to Beckett for Val's death before threatening him for his failure.<ref name="Solo" />

==Behind the scenes==
{{Quote|We talked about it a little bit, you know, I feel like Beckett saved her life at some point, and I feel like it may have been perilous for him to do that, so there's a sense of gratitude and loyalty there.|Thandiwe Newton about Val's backstory|<ref name="Solo DVD Commentary">''[[Solo: A Star Wars Story]]'' DVD commentary</ref>}}
[[File:GrappleGunVal.png|thumb|left|250px|Val's Grapple gun prop]]
Val was portrayed by [[Thandiwe Newton]] in the [[2018]] [[Star Wars Anthology Series|''Star Wars'' Anthology]] film ''[[Solo: A Star Wars Story]]''.<ref name="Solo" /> Newton's casting in the film was first announced on [[February 21]], [[2017]].<ref name="A New Star Wars Story Begins">{{SW|url=news/han-solo-smuggler-scoundrel-hero-new-star-wars-story-begins|text=Han Solo &ndash; Smuggler. Scoundrel. Hero. A New ''Star Wars'' Story Begins}}</ref> Her name was revealed with a set of promotional images sent out to various news websites alongside the teaser trailer.<ref name="Name">{{WebCite|url=http://www.jedinews.co.uk/film-music-tv/articles/solo-character-names-thandie-newton-phoebe-waller-bridge|text='Solo' Character Names For Thandie Newton & Phoebe Waller-Bridge|author=LaSalata, Justin|date=2018-02-05|work=''Jedi News''|archivedate=20200223191917}}</ref>

In the DVD commentary of ''Solo'', Newton revealed her thoughts about Val's backstory which she discussed with the filmmakers, stating that she likes to feel that Tobias Beckett saved Val from a dangerous situation at some point and that it was perilous for him to do that, earning Val's gratitude and loyalty in the process.<ref name="Solo DVD Commentary" /> The look of Val was inspired by pictures of female members of the [[Wikipedia:Viet Cong|Viet Cong]] and [[Wikipedia:Black Panther Party|Black Panther Party]], along with civil rights activist [[Wikipedia:Angela Davis|Angela Davis]].<ref name="Art of Solo">''[[The Art of Solo: A Star Wars Story]]''</ref>

Following the release of ''Solo'', co-writer [[Jon Kasdan]] expressed regret for having Val killed off early in the film, saying that Newton's acting was quite good and it would have been interesting having her playing a larger role in the film.<ref name="Jon's Twitter">{{Twitter|JonKasdan|status/1040529501870944256|[[Jon Kasdan]]|quote=In honor of the digital release of #SoloAStarWarsStory & in lieu of a commentary, here are 53 random factoids & notes about the making & writing for anybody who's interested. If you haven't seen the movie yet... maybe watch it before reading? Anyway, enjoy the movie & #MTFBWY|archivefile=File:Jon-Kasdan-SoloFacts-September-14-2018.png}}</ref> In a [[2021]] interview with [[Wikipedia:Inverse (website)|''Inverse'']], Newton expressed her outrage at Val's death early in the film due to her feelings of Val's significance as the first human black woman to have a notable role in a ''Star Wars'' film; Newton further elaborated that Val didn't die in the original script, with her falling out after the explosion, hinting at her survival, but this was ultimately changed when the scene was filmed, as the crew felt that the sequence would require a large set-piece and that it would be much easier to kill Val in the explosion.<ref name="NewtonsStatements">{{WebCite|url=https://www.inverse.com/entertainment/thandiwe-newton-interview-reminiscence-star-wars-westworld|text=Thandiwe Newton Speaks Out On Her Star Wars Death: "A Big Mistake"|author=Feldberg, Isaac|date=2021-08-25|work=[[Wikipedia:Inverse (website)|''Inverse'']]|archivedate=20210927135033}}</ref>

